\section{Model}{

For person \eqn{i} with moderator values \eqn{x_i} (no intercept column),
factors \eqn{\eta_i \mid x_i \sim N(\mu_i, \Sigma_i)} with
\itemize{
\item factor means \eqn{\mu_{id} = x_i'\gamma_d} (baseline mean 0);
\item factor variances \eqn{\sigma^2_{id} = \exp(\delta_{0d} + x_i'\delta_d)},
where \eqn{\delta_{0d} = 0} under variance identification;
\item factor correlations \eqn{\rho_i = \tanh(\zeta_0 + x_i'\zeta_1)}
(\code{cov_param = "fisherz"}) or covariances \eqn{\psi_i = c_0 + x_i'c_1}
(\code{cov_param = "linear"}).
}
Item \eqn{j} has loadings \eqn{\lambda_{ijd} = \lambda_{0jd} + x_i'\lambda_{1jd}}
and a moderator shift \eqn{\nu_{ij} = x_i'\beta_j}. For a categorical item
(binary or ordinal) with \eqn{K} categories,
\deqn{P(y_{ij} \ge k \mid \eta_i) = F(\nu_{ij} + \lambda_{ij}'\eta_i - \tau_{jk}),}
with \eqn{F} the logistic or standard normal distribution function, so a
positive \eqn{\beta} shifts all thresholds of the item down (uniform DIF).
The shift is the same for all thresholds of an item: the spacing of the
thresholds does not depend on the moderators. Category-specific threshold
moderation is not part of the model, and tests of \eqn{\beta} (Wald,
likelihood-ratio, score) do not test that larger alternative.
A continuous item is normal with mean \eqn{\nu_{0j} + \nu_{ij} + \lambda_{ij}'\eta_i}
and residual variance \eqn{\exp(\omega_{0j} + x_i'\omega_j)}.
}

\section{Specifying moderation}{

Item-level moderation (\code{loading_mod}, \code{threshold_mod}, \code{resvar_mod}) can be
given as \code{FALSE}/\code{NULL} (none), \code{TRUE} (all items by all moderators), a
character vector of item names (these items, by all moderators), a named
list mapping items to moderators (e.g. \code{list(item3 = "age")}), or a logical
items x moderators matrix. \code{loading_mod} also accepts an items x factors x
moderators array.

Factor-level moderation (\code{mean_mod}, \code{var_mod}) can be \code{TRUE}/\code{FALSE}, a
character vector of moderators (applied to all factors), a named list
mapping factors to moderators, or a logical factors x moderators matrix.
\code{cor_mod} can be \code{TRUE}/\code{FALSE}, a character vector of moderators (applied
to all factor pairs), or a logical pairs x moderators matrix.

Matrices and arrays must have exactly the model's dimensions. If they carry
dimension names (items, factors, moderators, factor pairs such as
\code{"f1,f2"}), these must match the model's names and are used to align the
entries; without names the entries are taken in the model's order. Nothing is
recycled.
}

\section{Identification}{

Fixing the baseline factor mean and variance (or a marker loading) does not
identify every moderation pattern. If a factor's mean and the
thresholds/intercepts of \emph{all} its items depend on the same moderator, a
shift of the factor mean cannot be told apart from equal shifts of all
items; likewise for the factor variance and \emph{all} loadings. Keep at least
one anchor item per factor and moderator whose parameters are not moderated.

Patterns that are exactly unidentified are refused with an error. For the
factor mean this includes models whose loadings are moderated as well, when
the products of the loading moderators with the mean moderators are
themselves absorbed by the threshold moderation: for a binary moderator
\eqn{z}, \eqn{\gamma \to \gamma + c} and
\eqn{\beta_j \to \beta_j - c(\lambda_{0j} + \lambda_{1j})} leave the
likelihood unchanged because \eqn{z^2 = z} (likewise for binary moderators
and all their interactions, or for \code{x} and \code{I(x^2)} both moderating the
thresholds). Patterns identified only through the moderated loadings (a
mean shift that is absorbed unless those loading effects are not zero), or
only through the functional form of the moderation (for example,
exponential variance against linear loadings for a continuous moderator)
give a warning. These checks look at each factor separately and are
sufficient conditions, not a proof of identification: \code{\link[=mofia]{mofia()}}
additionally checks that the information matrix at the solution is positive
definite. Which items are invariant is an
assumption: choosing anchors from the same data (for example with
\code{\link[=mofia_score_test]{mofia_score_test()}}) and then testing on those data is a form of
post-selection inference.

Cases without any observed item response are removed with a warning (they
do not contribute to the likelihood). Cases with some observed responses
contribute the likelihood of their observed items (missing at random).
}

\section{Parameter names}{

\code{tau[item,k]} thresholds; \code{nu0[item]} intercepts of continuous items;
\code{lambda0[item,factor]} baseline loadings; \code{lambda1[item,factor,moderator]}
loading moderation; \code{beta[item,moderator]} threshold/intercept moderation
(DIF); \code{omega0[item]}, \code{omega[item,moderator]} log residual variances of
continuous items; \code{gamma[factor,moderator]} factor-mean moderation;
\code{delta0[factor]} baseline log factor variance (marker identification only);
\code{delta[factor,moderator]} factor-variance moderation; \code{zcor0[f1,f2]},
\code{zcor1[f1,f2,moderator]} Fisher-z factor correlation (or \code{cov0}, \code{cov1} for
the linear covariance).
}

